   "content": "<p>Government of Pakistan</p><p>Foundations of the structure of the Government of Pakistan</p><p>The Islamic Republic of Pakistan is a democratic and federal parliamentary country in South Asia. After gaining independence on fourteenth August 1947, three constitutions, of 1956, 1962 and 1973, played a role in the constitutional evolution of Pakistan. At present the Constitution of 1973 is in force with several amendments. The foundations of the political system of the Government of Pakistan are as follows.</p><p>1. Ideology</p><p>At the time of the creation of Pakistan different forms of government were prevalent in different countries on the basis of different ideologies, such as democratic ideologies and socialist ideologies etc. Before the creation of Pakistan the system of government prevailing in the subcontinent of Pakistan and India was established on the pattern of the British government, and in Britain the system of government had been built on the basis of democratic ideologies. Thus the system of government of Pakistan was also established on the basis of democratic ideologies. Pakistan was achieved under an ideology which is also called the Ideology of Pakistan. Before independence Pakistan was explained through the slogan: “What does Pakistan mean? There is no god but Allah.” This ideology was basically presented by Allama Muhammad Iqbal in December 1930 at Allahabad while presiding over the annual session of the All India Muslim League. Through the Lahore Resolution of 23 March 1940 this concept was made clearer. That is, the creation of Pakistan took place on the basis of an ideology, and that ideology was the establishment of a separate homeland for the Muslims of Pakistan and India; the ideology of Islam, i.e. Pakistan would be a Muslim state established in the Muslim-majority areas of Pakistan and India. The basis of the constitution and government of this country would be placed on the opinion of the Muslims living in it. On 10 August 1947 the first session of the Constituent Assembly was held in Karachi, in which, speaking, the Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah said that Pakistan would be a free, sovereign Muslim state where there would be religious freedom, i.e. everyone would have complete freedom to go to their places of worship. In March 1949 …</p>",
