   "content": "<p>… the Objectives Resolution was passed, in which the sovereignty of Allah Almighty was acknowledged. In this way Islam was declared the official religion of the country in almost every constitution. In the Constitution of 1973 very many Islamic provisions have been included.</p><p>2. History</p><p>Pakistan has historically also been a unit. Today’s Pakistan is a step towards the ancient civilisation of South Asia, the Indus Valley civilisation (2500–1700 BC). The River Indus still holds the same position today that ancient history gave it. The River Indus is considered the bloodstream of Pakistan, and the historic civilisation of the Indus Valley is the identity of Pakistan. This region remained part of the Mughal Empire and, from about the middle of the nineteenth century, part of the British colonial system. The British government was based on parliamentary democracy; therefore governmental evolution in the subcontinent and the democratic form of government were founded on parliamentary democracy. To introduce the democratic system in the subcontinent the British government enforced various constitutional and legal reforms, for example the Government of India Acts of 1909, 1919 and 1935. Under these reforms law-making institutions were established, for whose formation an electoral system was gradually introduced and Indians were given representation in these institutions step by step. In this way the foundation of representative democracy was laid. The subcontinent gained independence from the British in 1947. The present political system of Pakistan is the evolutionary fruit and continuation of that democratic system. In historical terms these people faced the same kind of problems for a long time. Historically these people struggled jointly for the attainment of independence and bore hardships. Of the present provinces of Pakistan, Sindh, Punjab and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa (Sarhad) already existed, whereas Balochistan was given the status of a province after independence. The tribal identity still exists separately as before. This historical uniformity is reflected in today’s constitution and the structure of government.</p><p>3. Culture</p><p>Pakistan is a multi-ethnic country. There are four basic linguistic and ethnic groups, i.e. Punjabi, Sindhi, Pakhtun and Baloch. Sub-groups of these groups also exist. In Pakistan the constitution and the structure of government bear a deep influence of these linguistic and ethnic groups …</p>",
