   "content": "<p>… the democratic government made and enforced the Constitution of 1956. This constitution was prepared after considerable difficulties. Today’s constitution of Pakistan too was made and enforced by a democratic government with the consensus of the people’s representatives. Whenever military dictatorship was established in the country, they made amendments in this constitution of their own will. In Pakistan the problem of unemployment has always been very severe. Because in the first democratic period governments were caught up in political problems, special attention could not be paid to economic problems. In the later democratic periods importance was given to this issue. More opportunities of employment were created, self-employment schemes were launched, small businesses were encouraged and governments provided loans on easy terms. To control inflation hoarders were discouraged, prices were fixed and a system for their control was made, and governments established Utility Stores to provide articles of daily use at cheap prices. Long-term plans were prepared for the elimination of poverty. Several reforms were made in the field of agriculture. Zulfikar Ali Bhutto distributed the lands of the jagirdars among the cultivators and fixed a lower ceiling of land ownership; the Pakistan Agricultural Development Bank was established; Nawaz Sharif started the Green Tractor Scheme and loans were given to farmers on easy terms. Roads were built between fields and markets and concessions were given in the use of electricity in tube-wells. In democratic periods networks of industries were spread and industrial estates were established in the country. Reforms were made to improve the condition of labourers. In Bhutto’s government manpower was sent abroad for employment. Several reforms were made to solve the problems of employees, and their salaries and pensions were considerably increased.</p><p>The establishment of every democratic government is for solving public problems. That is why there are programmes for solving the people’s problems in the manifesto of every political party. Democratic leadership took steps to increase the literacy rate in the country. Zulfikar Ali Bhutto enforced educational reforms according to which primary education was made free, literacy centres were opened, several private schools and colleges were taken into national ownership, and several new universities were opened in the country, among which Islamia University Bahawalpur, Agricultural College Tando Jam, NED Engineering College Karachi etc. are worth mentioning. Later democratic governments too performed notable achievements in the field of education. Educational policies were issued for the improvement of education. In the field of health too democratic governments did good work. Health centres were opened in the country, medical facilities were increased, district hospitals were opened to reduce the rush in cities and the private sector was encouraged. To solve the problems of housing, standard and special hospitals at regional level …</p>",
