   "content": "<p>1. Economic Globalization</p><p>(According to the demands of the modern age, today the whole world has taken the shape of a global village. Scientific progress, modern means of communication, computers and the internet have played an important role in economic globalisation and international trade.) Politics and economics influence each other, and at the national level each is incomplete without the other. The study of those ways through which economics and government strategy in practice influence each other is called political economy, i.e. the mixture of economy and politics. Economic globalisation means the name of the increasing interdependence and relationship of the economies of states with one another. This dependence is the result of the movement, across the geographical boundaries of countries, of goods, services, technology and capital here and there. It is the name of the process of economic unity increasing among countries. In this way the concept of a market at the world level is emerging, and it consists of the globalisation of production, markets, competition, technology, corporations and industry etc.</p><p>Direct Foreign Aid</p><p>Aid means financial or material help which is generally given by a country or a world institution in a state of crisis. Foreign aid is that military or economic help which one country gives to another country. Japan, Britain and America are leading among the aid-giving countries. There are several forms of direct foreign aid, e.g. providing cash, goods, services or technical skill etc. It can be two-sided, i.e. one country provides it to another, or a multinational one which provides it to a group of countries. Several financial and private institutions also provide world aid. Foreign aid is either on the basis of human sympathy or for the attainment of one’s own national interests.</p><p>Economic Globalization</p><p>Economic globalisation means that economics has taken a world form, and with regard to economics this world is like a village. In fact it is a collection of several things, i.e. the rapid spread of communications, the rapid spread of trade, of capital …</p>",
