   "content": "<p>Pakistan Bureau of Statistics.</p><p>During 1999-2000 America remained the biggest market for Pakistani goods and absorbed about 21 percent of all sales. Britain, Hong Kong and Germany are also important buyers, and the share of each was about 7 percent, while the share of the United Arab Emirates remained 5 percent. In the year 1999 Pakistan showed a deficit of 1.4 billion US dollars in trade. During financial year 2007 the trade deficit was 9.7 billion US dollars, while during the year 2008 it remained 15 billion US dollars.</p><p>Pakistan’s imports are machinery, oil, chemicals and the tools of transport. Among them eight percent each from America and Japan, and seven percent each from Malaysia, Saudi Arabia and the Emirates. In the year 2003 the European Union remained Pakistan’s biggest trading partner, and the European Union absorbed one-third of Pakistan’s exports. Pakistan is a member of the World Trade Organization (WTO). In the same way it is an active member of a regional organisation, the ECO, and is also an important member of SAARC. Pakistan’s regional trading partners are Bangladesh, India and Sri Lanka. To reduce the trade deficit Pakistan increased the tariff on several goods. Pakistan is moving towards free-trade agreements with several friendly countries, i.e. Malaysia, Sri Lanka and China.</p><p>Impacts of International Trade Agreements on Pakistan</p><p>Pakistan is a member of the World Trade Organization (WTO). This institution has the following effects on Pakistan’s politics and economy:</p>",
