   "content": "<p>6. North Korean Atomic Dispute</p><p>Like Iran, North Korea too is a target of Western criticism, because according to them North Korea, despite being a member of the treaty on the non-proliferation of atomic materials, is trying to make atomic weapons. According to the American president Obama, North Korea is not fulfilling its duties regarding the non-proliferation treaty. In October 2006 North Korea carried out an underground atomic explosion and also formally announced it. Scientific institutions of the Western world confirmed it too. In negotiations held in October 2003 with Chinese and American officials in Beijing, the officials of North Korea admitted that they have atomic weapons. Along with this they also threatened that North Korea intends to obtain more atomic weapons and export them. North Korea has also obtained plutonium, which proves the existence of atomic weapons.</p><p>7. Pak-India Affairs</p><p>Pakistan and India are traditional rivals and several serious problems exist between them. The problems in which the path of negotiations was adopted, only those problems were resolved; therefore there is a need that for the solution of other problems too the path of negotiations be adopted:</p><p>Pak-India dispute over the division of rivers:</p><p>At this time there is a dispute between Hindustan and Pakistan over three dams under construction in Hindustan on Pakistan’s water. These three projects include the Baglihar Dam, the Wular Barrage and the Kishanganga Dam, which are being built on the water coming to Pakistan. On one side Pakistan already faces a shortage of water, while on the other side India has started a plan to build dams on the rivers coming into Pakistan’s share. Through occupied Kashmir India has the facility of access to these rivers. This is a violation of the Indus Waters Treaty. Pakistan has protested against the construction of these dams. Pakistan’s Neelum–Jhelum project too can be affected by these projects. Pakistan’s position is that India can use these dams as a weapon. Negotiation on these is continuing, and the United Nations too is being used for the resolution of this issue.</p>",
