   "content": "<p>The Pak-India wars of 1965 and 1971:</p><p>After the devastation of the 1965 war between Pakistan and India, at the wish of the government of the former Soviet Union, talks took place in the Russian city of Tashkent and the Tashkent agreement was settled. In the same way, after the 1971 battle, successful negotiations took place between Pakistan and India in the Indian city of Simla and a peace agreement was settled between the two countries. That is, countries realise that problems should be solved through talks and problems cannot be solved through war, but there is a need to act on this thinking.</p><p>Pak-India and Kashmir:</p><p>Kashmir is a grave and dangerous issue between the two countries which can engulf in atomic war not only this region but the whole world. Between Pakistan and India several wars have taken place, or nearly taken place, because of this issue. The whole world wishes that the two countries resolve this issue through mutual talks, but because of India’s stubbornness the process of negotiations has not yet been started successfully.</p><p>Use of Negotiations to Settle International Disputes</p><p>In the history of the world innumerable conflicts are mentioned which were either resolved through negotiation or attempts were made to resolve them. After the First World War several international conflicts raised their heads. After the end of the First World War the peace conference was held near the French city of Paris. After considerable thought and negotiation, several important problems and conflicts were resolved.</p><p>The government of North Korea in 1993-94 withdrew from the treaty on the non-proliferation of atomic materials (NPT) and announced keeping its atomic programme going. The American President Bill Clinton adopted the policy of solving this issue through negotiation …</p>",
