   "content": "<p>… the winning party or parties form the government according to law. Today at the world level the way of reaching government is support from the majority of the public. When a party forms the government, it then tries through governmental power to give practical shape to its programme. If any party fails to give practical form to its programme, it has to face a bad defeat in the next elections. Throughout the world this is the popular method of government formation. A government formed through political parties is a people’s government.</p><p>For the formation of any effective and stable political system and democratic society the existence of an opposition is of extreme importance. The opposition reminds the governing party of its manifesto, programme and the promises made to the public. If the governing party exceeds its powers and legal limits, the opposition criticises it severely and tries to keep it within its sphere. It is a check on the governing party. The tragedy in developing countries is that neither does the governing party tolerate the opposition nor does the opposition play its legitimate role; rather the aim of the opposition is to defame the governing party by any means, and their one aim is “opposition for the sake of opposition”.</p><p>Political parties play a two-way role. On one side they convey the problems of the public to the government, while on the other … and activities to the public. They are a means of mutual contact, and this contact is of importance for the stability and improvement of the political system. In a state, especially in multi-ethnic states, sometimes small ethnic groups are not able to obtain representation as a result of general elections. They have no representation in national politics and their problems are not solved. In such circumstances such small and unrepresented ethnic groups become pressure groups and try to get their problems solved from this same platform. Such groups ultimately change into political parties. These parties then present the problems of these nationalities effectively.</p><p>Structure/Composition of Political Parties</p><p>A political party is a regular and most important part of the political system. It works as a subsystem within the political system. In every state the composition and structure of a political party is different, but some of its elements …</p>",
