     "text": "Political parties without members are like a hollow body. A political party prepares a programme and conveys it to the public. If there is effect in this programme, people start taking interest in it and obtain membership of that political party. This membership is voluntary and they are not given any kind of salary; rather, to run party affairs, these same volunteers give donations from their own pockets. These volunteers follow the instructions of the leadership. These people with great zeal and passion try to spread the programme and ideology of their party among the public so that party membership increases. Members elect the leadership. These same volunteers take part actively in elections, meetings, processions and demonstrations etc. These volunteers of the party offer every kind of sacrifice for their party. Membership is given according to the constitution of the party. The membership of a volunteer can be suspended when he violates the manifesto and constitution of the party. The success of parties depends on active members (Activists). Collecting the greatest number of volunteer members depends on the character of the party, the learning and personality of the leadership and the party manifesto. If the party programme mirrors the psychology and trends of the citizens of that country and the party’s means of communication are effective, then the greatest number of people will join the party. The uniformity of ideology among the volunteers and the struggle for the ideology become the cause of party unity. The party remains organised and the volunteers play a key and critical role in elections.",
