   "content": "<p>Glossary</p><p>(Alif)</p><p>Ishtimaliyat (Socialism):</p><p>Ishtirakiyat and Ishtimaliyat (Socialism and Communism): Ishtirakiyat, i.e. socialism, means a political and economic ideology in which the important means of production and income are taken into national or collective ownership. Everyone is given remuneration according to his work. Work is taken from everyone according to his ability. Man cannot exploit man, and the class system is ended, considering it an insult to humanity. This system is in China.</p><p>Ishtimaliyat (Communism): Ishtimaliyat or communism is such a political and economic ideology in which all means of production and income are taken into national or collective ownership. Everyone is given remuneration according to his need. Work is taken from everyone according to his ability. Man cannot exploit man, and the class system is ended because it is an insult to humanity. This system was in the former Soviet Union.</p><p>Infiradiyat (Individualism): When the state interferes as little as possible in the affairs of citizens and citizens have more freedom.</p><p>Irtiqa (Evolution): Gradual development, e.g. the growth of a human or a plant.</p><p>Anjuman (Association): Human organisations or institutions; an organised gathering of individuals to solve some common matter collectively.</p><p>Ifadiyat (Utility): The benefit of some work, thing, individual or institution.</p><p>Isti‘dad (Potential): Hidden abilities in an individual.</p><p>Arastu (Aristotle): A Greek philosopher of about 400 BC who is called the founder of political science.</p><p>Intikhab (Election): When the public or an institution does an appointment to some office on the basis of majority opinion, this is election.</p><p>Ita‘at (Obedience): To obey, accept, act upon, accept willingly.</p><p>Inqilab (Revolution): Immediate political change by violent methods.</p><p>Imtiyaz (Discrimination): Differentiation.</p><p>Islah (Reforms): Making some thing or programme more effective and useful by positive change.</p><p>Ihya (Renaissance): Reviving some absent or forgotten thing or principle.</p>",
