   "content": "<p>Kabina (Cabinet (continued)): … (Cabinet) for advice on state affairs; therefore the council of ministers was given the name of cabinet.</p><p>Kal-‘adam (Ban, null and void): Non-existent, extinct; as if it does not exist.</p><p>Convention (Convention): A joint treaty of several countries.</p><p>(Gaf)</p><p>Globalization (Globalization): The world changing into one village.</p><p>(Lam)</p><p>Latini (Latin): The ancient Greek language.</p><p>Lachakdar (Flexible): Soft; in which change is possible with ease.</p><p>(Mim)</p><p>Ma‘ashi (Economic): Economic; concerned with economy; activities of production, trade, income and revenue and finance etc.</p><p>Mu‘ashrati (Social): That aspect of human life which is called social on the basis of mutual relations and way of living.</p><p>Mu‘ashra (Society): A group of humans living together on a permanent basis for the fulfilment of mutual needs is called society.</p><p>Mu‘ahida (Treaty): The parties agreeing on some matter or making a pledge.</p><p>Muqtadir (Sovereign): That individual or institution which exercises the highest powers.</p><p>Mutlaq (Absolute): Possessor of full powers.</p><p>Maqami hukumat (Local Government): Institutions subordinate to the central government which run affairs at the local level, in cities, villages and rural areas, by using government powers to some extent through elected representatives, e.g. district and tehsil councils etc. It is also given the name of basic democracy.</p><p>Ma‘ishat (Economy): The economic condition of a country.</p><p>Madar-sari (Matriarchal): That social system in which a woman is the head of the family.</p><p>Millat (Millat): A comprehensive nationhood, e.g. despite the citizenship of different states, all Muslims are one millat.</p><p>Mauroosi (Hereditary): From generation to generation.</p><p>Mantiqi (Logical): With strong and solid arguments.</p>",
